{
  "title": "How compound interest works",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A little extra grows",
      "text": "Imagine putting $100 in a savings account. The bank pays you interest: a little extra money for keeping your savings there. What happens when that extra money stays in the account?",
      "imagePrompt": "A savings account shown as a jar containing $100, with a small amount of interest being added."
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest earns interest",
      "text": "When interest is added to your balance, future interest can be earned on that extra money too. This is compound interest: your original money and the interest already added both help your balance grow.",
      "imagePrompt": "A jar with original savings and a separate small pile labeled interest; arrows show both contributing to new interest."
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch two years unfold",
      "text": "Suppose you save $100 at 5% interest per year, with interest added yearly. After one year, you have $105. In the second year, you earn $5.25, bringing the balance to $110.25. That extra 25 cents came from interest earning interest.",
      "imagePrompt": "Two steps showing a savings balance growing from $100 to $105 after year one, then to $110.25 after year two, with the extra 25 cents highlighted."
    },
    {
      "title": "Time makes a difference",
      "text": "Like a snowball collecting more snow as it rolls, a balance can grow faster as interest builds on interest. The rate, how often interest is added, and how long you leave the money there all affect the result.",
      "imagePrompt": "A small snowball rolling downhill and growing larger, alongside a savings balance growing over time."
    },
    {
      "title": "It can work against you",
      "text": "Compounding can also increase debt, such as an unpaid credit card balance, when interest is charged on interest. Account terms, fees, payments, and changing rates matter. For your situation, consider asking a qualified financial professional.",
      "imagePrompt": null
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "Liam leaves his savings interest in his account. Why might he earn more interest in the next year, even if the rate stays the same?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "His earlier interest is now part of the balance earning interest.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Right. The interest already added can earn interest too."
      },
      {
        "text": "His bank must raise the interest rate every year.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. The balance can earn more even when the rate stays the same."
      },
      {
        "text": "His original deposit automatically doubles every year.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Compounding adds interest, but it does not automatically double a deposit each year."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Interest adds money to your savings.",
    "Compound interest lets earlier interest earn interest too.",
    "Time and account terms affect growth; compounding can also increase debt."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Personal Finance",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "How do savings account interest rates work?",
      "How is credit card interest charged?",
      "What is the difference between simple and compound interest?"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "title": "Fórmula de Bhaskara",
  "screens": [
    {
